# Unshare configuration template
# See: man unshare(1)

# This template creates an isolated sandbox environment using Linux namespaces

# --net: Create a new network namespace
# Isolates the process from the host network stack, providing network isolation.
# The process will have its own network interfaces, routing tables, and firewall rules.
# Used in combination with slirp4netns to provide user-mode networking.
--net

# --user: Create a new user namespace
# Allows unprivileged users to have root capabilities within the namespace.
# This is essential for creating other namespaces without requiring actual root privileges.
# Requires kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=1 on some systems.
--user

# -r (--map-root-user): Map the current user to root inside the namespace
# Maps the current UID/GID to 0 (root) within the user namespace.
# This allows the sandboxed process to perform privileged operations within its namespace
# without having actual root privileges on the host system.
-r

# --mount: Create a new mount namespace
# Isolates the filesystem mount points from the host.
# Changes to mounts inside the namespace won't affect the host or other namespaces.
# Used with mount --bind to create custom filesystem layouts.
--mount

# --propagation private: Set mount propagation to private
# Prevents mount/unmount events from propagating between the namespace and the host.
# This ensures complete mount isolation and prevents interference with the host filesystem.
--propagation private

# --pid: Create a new PID namespace
# Isolates the process ID number space, making the sandboxed process tree independent.
# The first process in the namespace becomes PID 1 (init-like process).
# Requires --fork to work correctly.
--pid

# --fork: Fork a new process before applying namespaces
# Required when using --pid to ensure the calling process doesn't become PID 1.
# The parent process maintains the original namespaces while the child runs in the new ones.
--fork

# --kill-child: Send SIGKILL to the child when the parent exits
# Ensures cleanup of the sandboxed process if the parent terminates unexpectedly.
# Prevents orphaned processes from continuing to run after the sandbox manager exits.
--kill-child

